  "content": {
    "id": "p141-q33-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p141-q33-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that in general $f(g(x))\\ne g(f(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "At $x=3$, $f(g(3))=2$, whereas $g(f(3))=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{\\sqrt{3}-2}<0$. Thus the compositions are not generally equal.",
          "worked": "At $x=3$, both compositions are defined.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(3))&=\\sqrt{\\frac{3+1}{3-2}}\\\\f(g(3))&=2,\\\\g(f(3))&=g(\\sqrt{3})\\\\g(f(3))&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{\\sqrt{3}-2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $0<\\sqrt{3}<2$, the last fraction has a positive numerator and a negative denominator. Hence $g(f(3))<0$, whereas $f(g(3))=2$. The two compositions are therefore not generally equal.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-q33-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the domain and range of $f\\circ g$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p141-q33-b-short-domain",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Domain: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(-\\infty,-1]\\cup(2,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p141-q33-b-short-range",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Range: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p141-q33-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Require ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g(x)\\ge0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", retaining ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x\\ne2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". Apply the square root to the permitted outputs of ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "; the value ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is never attained.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$(f\\circ g)(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{x-2}}.$$\n\nThe inner function requires $x\\ne2$, and the outer square root requires $\\frac{x+1}{x-2}\\ge0$. For $x<2$, the denominator is negative, so the numerator must be nonpositive, giving $x\\le-1$. For $x>2$, both numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore,\n\n$$D_{f\\circ g}=(-\\infty,-1]\\cup(2,\\infty).$$\n\nTo find the permitted inner outputs, write\n\n$$\\begin{align*}g(x)&=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}\\\\g(x)&=1+\\frac{3}{x-2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $x\\le-1$, the denominator runs through $(-\\infty,-3]$, so $\\frac{3}{x-2}$ runs through $[-1,0)$. Thus $g(x)$ takes every value in $[0,1)$, and applying $f$ gives every value in $[0,1)$.\n\nFor $x>2$, the term $\\frac{3}{x-2}$ takes every positive value. Thus $g(x)$ takes every value in $(1,\\infty)$, and applying $f$ gives every value in $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nHence,\n\n$$R_{f\\circ g}=[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-q33-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the domain and range of $g\\circ f$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p141-q33-c-short-domain",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Domain: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "[0,4)\\cup(4,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p141-q33-c-short-range",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Range: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(-\\infty,-\\tfrac12]\\cup(1,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p141-q33-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Put ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "t=\\sqrt{x}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". Map the permitted inner-output intervals ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "[0,2)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(2,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " through ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "g(t)=1+\\frac{3}{t-2}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$(g\\circ f)(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+1}{\\sqrt{x}-2}.$$\n\nThe inner square root requires $x\\ge0$. Its output must also satisfy $\\sqrt{x}\\ne2$, so $x\\ne4$. Therefore,\n\n$$D_{g\\circ f}=[0,4)\\cup(4,\\infty).$$\n\nPut $t=\\sqrt{x}$. The permitted inner outputs are $t\\in[0,2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$, and\n\n$$\\begin{align*}g(t)&=\\frac{t+1}{t-2}\\\\g(t)&=1+\\frac{3}{t-2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $0\\le t<2$, the denominator takes every value in $[-2,0)$. Thus $\\frac{3}{t-2}$ takes every value in $(-\\infty,-\\frac32]$, and $g(t)$ takes every value in $(-\\infty,-\\frac12]$.\n\nFor $t>2$, the term $\\frac{3}{t-2}$ takes every positive value, so $g(t)$ takes every value in $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nHence,\n\n$$R_{g\\circ f}=(-\\infty,-\\tfrac12]\\cup(1,\\infty).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      }
    ]
